            <name>wikivoyage</name>
            <Placemark id="3682">
                <name>Hauptkirche St. Michaelis </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_The five main churches of Hamburg &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://www.st-michaelis.de/  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : The youngest and most famous of the five is also the only baroque church. This building is not the original St. Michaelis - that one (built 1647-1669) was destroyed by a lightning strike in 1750. It was not until 1786 that the present church was erected on the site. From the tower (450 steps, or take the elevator) you'll have a great view over the city. Entry to the church is free, but the tower and the crypt cost extra (around €5/person). &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3681">
                    <coordinates>9.978889,53.548333,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3684">
                <name>Hauptkirche St. Jacobi </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_The five main churches of Hamburg &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Jakobikirkhof  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : St. Jacobi has been a parish since the 12th century, when it was not even within Hamburg's then city walls. The present-day church is a reconstruction of the late medieval one from the 15th century, which was destroyed during the Second World War. The reconstruction afforded St. Jacobi with a visibly modern roof and steeple. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3683">
                    <coordinates>10.000556,53.550278,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3686">
                <name>Hauptkirche St. Petri </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_The five main churches of Hamburg &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Bergstraße  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : St. Petri was a cathedral as early as the 12th century or possibly even earlier. As with other ''Hauptkirchen'', the present version is one of many successive, and was only completed in 1878 after the great fire in 1842 consumed its predecessor. In turn, however, St. Petri survived the Second World War relatively intact. Of note are the large ornate door handles in the shape of lion heads, which have survived both the fire and the war, and the bells, several of which date back to the 16th century. The tower can be climbed for a fee and offers a great view of Hamburg, but the climb is long and not recommended for people suffering from high anxiety. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3685">
                    <coordinates>9.996389,53.550278,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3688">
                <name>Hauptkirche St. Nikolai </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_The five main churches of Hamburg &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : All five main churches of Hamburg were damaged in World War II. But in contrast to the other four, St. Nikolai has not been re-erected making it a memorial against war. The steeple is still standing and visitors can take an elevator to the top for a view of the city &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3687">
                    <coordinates>9.990556,53.5475,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3690">
                <name>Hauptkirche St. Katharinen </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_The five main churches of Hamburg &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Situated on an island, it traditionally served as the church of the seamen. The base of its spire, dating from the 13th century, is the second oldest building preserved in the city. The church is also famous for its centuries-old organ, played by Bach when he was visiting the city. The instrument was actually destroyed to a large extent by the wartime bombings, but has been meticulously rebuilt and is playable again. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3689">
                    <coordinates>9.994375,53.546,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3692">
                <name>Hulbe-Haus </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around Mönckebergstraße &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Dating from the beginning of the 20th century as most buildings around, but looking much older. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3691">
                    <coordinates>9.99752,53.5505,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3694">
                <name>Radio Hamburg </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around Mönckebergstraße &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Under the building you can visit the remains of the bishops tower, from the 11th century. On the other side of the road, you can see excavations in progress, seeking the remains of the small fortress Hammaburg, which was erected in the 9th century giving Hamburg its name. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3693">
                    <coordinates>10.0027,53.5522,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3696">
                <name>Rathaus </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around city hall &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Rathausmarkt 1  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Hamburg's impressive city hall was built in 1897 out of sandstone in neo-Renaissance style, including a 112-m tower. The square in front of the city hall is the Rathausmarkt, hosting many events especially in summer. Inside there are several magnificent halls used for representative purposes and sittings of government and parliament. These can be visited in guided tours (M-Th 10:00-15:15, F-Su 10:00-13:15, half-hourly in German, hourly in English and French. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3695">
                    <coordinates>9.99245,53.55049,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3698">
                <name>Hamburger Börse </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around city hall &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Between the buildings, there is a little courtyard called Rathaushof with its fountain, Hygieia-Brunnen. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3697">
                    <coordinates>9.99156,53.54996,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3700">
                <name>Wall-Anlagen </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around St. Nikolai &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : The western part of the former city wall is nowadays a park area surrounding the inner city as a semicircle, ranging from the Landungsbrücken (Helgoländer Allee) up to Binnenalster. In the Wallanlagen there's a big playground and in the winter an ice rink. There also the impressive Hanseatic Courthouse building. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3699">
                    <coordinates>9.981,53.5571,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3702">
                <name>Patriotische Gesellschaft </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around St. Nikolai &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : The building of the Patriotic Society was erected following the great fire of 1842 on the former site of the old town hall of Hamburg. Built using the most modern technology at that time, including concrete substructure, and featuring modern techology such as flush toilets, it was nevertheless kept in a style that was an interpretation of the surrounding medieval architectural heritage. Of note inside is the large hanging clock by Aloys Denoth. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3701">
                    <coordinates>9.99229,53.54853,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3704">
                <name>Trostbrücke </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around St. Nikolai &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : It has statues of Graf Adolf III and Bishop Ansgar on both sides. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3703">
                    <coordinates>9.99225,53.54815,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3706">
                <name>Zollenbrücke </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around St. Nikolai &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Hamburg's oldest remaining bridge from the 17th century &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3705">
                    <coordinates>9.99362,53.54772,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="3708">
                <name>Chilehaus </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around St. Nikolai &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : The house, depicting the form of a ship, is probably the best example of the 1920s style of &quot;Kontorhaus&quot; architecture. Large office buildings are displayed in the typical, northern red brick style. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="3707">
                    <coordinates>10.003,53.5483,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
