                description  : '''Buxtehude''' &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#170</styleUrl>
                <Point id="4205">
                    <coordinates>9.7003941,53.4767351,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="4208">
                <name>Helgoland</name>
                <description>
                category  : Go next &lt;br&gt;
                address  :  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : '''Helgoland''' — Germany's most off-shore North Sea island. Reachable by [http://www.helgoline.de express ferry] from St. Pauli Landungsbrücken. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#170</styleUrl>
                <Point id="4207">
                    <coordinates>7.8876314,54.1820608,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="4210">
                <name>Itzehoe</name>
                <description>
                category  : Go next &lt;br&gt;
                address  :  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : '''Itzehoe''' &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#170</styleUrl>
                <Point id="4209">
                    <coordinates>9.5172441,53.919448,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="4212">
                <name>Kiel</name>
                <description>
                category  : Go next &lt;br&gt;
                address  :  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : '''Kiel''' — Kiel's main tourist attraction is the &quot;Kieler Woche&quot; (Kiel Week) at the end of June, the largest sailing event of the world and one of Germany's largest festivals. Kiel is also notable as one of the most important historic bases of the German Navy as well as its U-Boats, and several sights related to this history can be viewed in Kiel and the suburb of Laboe. Trains to Kiel leave at least once per hour [http://reiseauskunft.bahn.de/bin/query.exe/en?S=Hamburg&amp;Z=Kiel from Hamburg main station]{{Dead link|date=January 2025 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }} and the trip takes about an hour. A trip to Kiel on the Autobahn (A7) takes about an hour, too. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#170</styleUrl>
                <Point id="4211">
                    <coordinates>10.135555,54.3227085,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="4214">
                <name>Lübeck</name>
                <description>
                category  : Go next &lt;br&gt;
                address  :  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : '''Lübeck''' — The city is connected to the Baltic Sea by the Trave river. The old city (Altstadt) survived from medieval times and is part of the UNESCO World Heritage List. About 60&amp;nbsp;km northeast of Hamburg, direct trains [http://reiseauskunft.bahn.de/bin/query.exe/en?S=Hamburg&amp;Z=L%FCbeck leave from main station]{{Dead link|date=January 2025 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }} every hour. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#170</styleUrl>
                <Point id="4213">
                    <coordinates>10.684738,53.866444,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="4216">
                <name>Lüneburg</name>
                <description>
                category  : Go next &lt;br&gt;
                address  :  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : '''Lüneburg''' — A city in Lower Saxony, about 50&amp;nbsp;km southeast of Hamburg. Like Lübeck, Lüneburg's old town has kept a medieval look with old buildings and narrow streets. The town is situated in the beautiful Lüneburger Heide. South of Hamburg, direct trains [http://reiseauskunft.bahn.de/bin/query.exe/en?S=Hamburg&amp;Z=L%FCneburg leave from main station]{{Dead link|date=January 2025 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }} every hour. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#170</styleUrl>
                <Point id="4215">
                    <coordinates>10.407855,53.248706,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="4218">
                <name>Travemünde</name>
                <description>
                category  : Go next &lt;br&gt;
                address  :  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : '''Travemünde''' – The old town at the Baltic Sea maintained its sleepy character despite the fact that at weekends there are loads of tourists. It also serves as the port of Lübeck and has done so since the Middle Ages. There are various connections throughout the Baltic, as well as cruises that leave from this port &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#170</styleUrl>
                <Point id="4217">
                    <coordinates>10.8587192,53.939609,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="4220">
                <name>Stade</name>
                <description>
                category  : Go next &lt;br&gt;
                address  :  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : '''Altes Land''' — The region is the biggest connected fruit growing area of Central Europe and one of the furthest north in the world. The main product grown in this area is apples in astounding variety. Altes Land is an area of marshland south of the river Elbe in Hamburg and Lower Saxony around the old towns of Stade, Buxtehude, and Jork. A characteristic feature is the richly-decorated farmhouses with their elaborate gateways. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#170</styleUrl>
                <Point id="4219">
                    <coordinates>9.475438,53.599794,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="4222">
                <name>Ahrensburg</name>
                <description>
                category  : Go next &lt;br&gt;
                address  :  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : '''Ahrensburg''' — Northeastern suburb &lt;!--that being the operative term, I wonder if we can't better deal with this place within Hamburg or as a district of the city...--&gt; of Hamburg, situated in Stormarn district. Its outstanding sight is the Renaissance castle dating from 1595. Ahrensburg is easily accessible by car and train (Hamburg public transport). &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#170</styleUrl>
                <Point id="4221">
                    <coordinates>10.2358999,53.6736529,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="4224">
                <name>Sankt Peter-Ording</name>
                <description>
                category  : Go next &lt;br&gt;
                address  :  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : '''Sankt Peter-Ording''' — Germany's most popular tourist destination by the sea. Features a broad surfer's beach and stilt houses. Easily accessible by car (Autobahn 23, about 120&amp;nbsp;km) and [http://reiseauskunft.bahn.de/bin/query.exe/en?S=Hamburg&amp;Z=Bad%20St%20Peter-Ording train]{{Dead link|date=January 2025 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#170</styleUrl>
                <Point id="4223">
                    <coordinates>8.6254937,54.3172664,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
        </Folder>
